                <h2>How Long Does Top-up Take to Arrive?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: Online payments typically arrive within 5-30 minutes; bank transfers take 1-3 business days.</strong></p>
<p>If you use credit cards or PayPal directly on Meta, Google, or TikTok ad platforms, funds usually arrive within minutes. However, if you go through third-party agents, arrival time depends on their financial processing speed. Always screenshot transaction receipts and record top-up timestamps for future verification.</p>
<p><strong>Common pitfall:</strong> Some agents operate on a "spend first, settle later" model. Your account balance may appear normal while actual spend has already overdrafted. Always prioritize real-time spend data from the platform backend over agent-provided balance figures.</p>
<h2>What to Do When Platform Spend Doesn't Match Your Bill?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: Platform backend data is authoritative; billing amounts are reference only.</strong></p>
<p>Meta Ads Manager and Google Ads display real-time spend data. Minor discrepancies (1-2%) due to timezone settings or currency conversion are normal. However, if variance exceeds 5%, investigate these causes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timezone differences</strong>: Many teams overlook this. Platforms typically use UTC time while financial bills may use local timezone, causing daily spend misalignment</li>
<li><strong>Data delays</strong>: Some platforms have 24-48 hour retrospective adjustments; use final confirmed spend data when reconciling monthly bills</li>
<li><strong>Account structure issues</strong>: With multiple sub-accounts or agency accounts, parent account aggregation may have calculation discrepancies</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Export platform backend spend details on a fixed monthly schedule and cross-check against internal financial records. Don't rely solely on billing figures for judgment.</p>
<h2>Can You Correct Invoice Details After Submission?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: Reissuance is possible within 30 days of invoicing; modifications are nearly impossible after that period.</strong></p>
<p>Most advertising platforms issue invoices after the billing cycle ends—for example, Meta issues monthly invoices. Once an invoice is generated and sent, correcting company names, tax IDs, or business addresses becomes extremely difficult.</p>
<p><strong>Actionable advice:</strong><br />
- Enter correct company registration names and tax IDs during account setup; avoid abbreviations<br />
- For multi-entity operations, confirm which entity bears advertising costs and bind corresponding information in the backend in advance<br />
- After invoice applications are submitted, verify information on electronic invoices immediately and contact platform support within 24 hours if issues are found</p>
<h2>How to Reconcile Multi-Platform Advertising Spend?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: Consolidate using attribution dimensions consistently; don't mix platform metrics.</strong></p>
<p>Overseas teams typically operate Meta, Google, TikTok, and other platforms simultaneously. Each platform has different data formats, timezones, and currency units. The key to unified reconciliation is establishing your own internal spend ledger with four standard fields: spend date, account, spend amount (USD), and corresponding RMB conversion.</p>
<p>In practice, use tools like Feishu spreadsheets or Qingflow to build a simple spend summary template. Manually export reports from each platform backend at the beginning of each month and fill into the unified template. If managing more than 10 accounts, consider integrating third-party attribution platforms like DataTower or AppGrowing for automatic data aggregation.</p>
<h2>How to Handle Currency Fluctuation Impact on Reconciliation?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: Use fixed monthly exchange rate for external reporting; use transaction-day rates for internal bookkeeping.</strong></p>
<p>Advertising platforms are mostly USD-denominated while domestic finance books in RMB. Retroactively calculating monthly spend using month-end exchange rates distorts reports. Correct approach:</p>
<ul>
<li>Finance entries use the bank's spot sell rate on the day of transaction</li>
<li>Monthly summary reports can use a fixed reference rate, but must note "for reference only"</li>
<li>For large spend (exceeding $100,000 monthly), confirm with platforms or agents whether forward exchange services are available</li>
</ul>
<h2>Which Data Should You Trust: Agent Bills or Platform Backend?</h2>
<p><strong>Core judgment: For financial matters, always prioritize official platform data; agent data is for reference only.</strong></p>
<p>Some teams习惯 rely on agents for top-ups and bill viewing, but agent backend failures or data delays cause misjudgment. For example, agent shows sufficient balance while platform has already paused campaigns, or agent bills show lower spend while overdraft has actually occurred.</p>
<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Financial reconciliation must use official platform backend data as authoritative. Agent-provided bills are for internal reference only. When discrepancies are found, immediately verify with agents and retain all communication records.</p>
